questions.solution:
First, calculate the vertical height h of water rise:
h = \frac{2\text{T}\cos\theta_c}{\rho g r}

In CGS units:
\text{T} = 70\text{ dyn/cm}, \rho = 1\text{ g/cm}^3, g = 980\text{ cm/s}^2, r = 0.1\text{ mm} = 0.01\text{ cm}, \theta_c = 0^\circ

h = \frac{2 \times 70 \times 1}{1 \times 980 \times 0.01} = \frac{140}{9.8} = \frac{100}{7}\text{ cm}

Since the tube is inclined at angle \alpha = 30^\circ with the vertical, the length of the liquid column along the tube \ell is:
\ell = \frac{h}{\cos 30^\circ} = \frac{100/7}{\sqrt{3}/2} = \frac{200}{7\sqrt{3}} \approx 16.49\text{ cm}

Among the given choices, \frac{82}{5} = 16.4\text{ cm} is the closest match.

Electric field between the plates:
\text{E} = \frac{\text{V}}{d} = \frac{5}{0.5 \times 10^{-3}} = 10^4\text{ V/m}

Dipole moment:
p = q \cdot (2a) = (2 \times 10^{-6}\text{ C}) \times (0.5 \times 10^{-6}\text{ m}) = 10^{-12}\text{ C}\cdot\text{m}

Torque acting on the dipole rotated by \theta = 30^\circ:
\tau = p\text{E}\sin\theta = (10^{-12}) \times (10^4) \times \sin 30^\circ = 10^{-8} \times \frac{1}{2} = 5 \times 10^{-9}\text{ N}\cdot\text{m}

Let the outputs of the two intermediate gates be \text{Y}_1 and \text{Y}_2:
- Top AND gate: \text{Y}_1 = \text{A} \cdot \text{B}
- Bottom OR gate: Inputs are \bar{\text{A}} (from inverter) and \text{B}. So \text{Y}_2 = \bar{\text{A}} + \text{B}
- Final NAND gate: \text{Y} = \overline{\text{Y}_1 \cdot \text{Y}_2} = \overline{(\text{A}\text{B}) \cdot (\bar{\text{A}} + \text{B})} = \overline{\text{A}\text{B}\bar{\text{A}} + \text{A}\text{B}\text{B}} = \overline{0 + \text{A}\text{B}} = \overline{\text{A}\text{B}}

Thus \text{Y} = 0 when \text{A}\text{B} = 1, which means \text{A} = 1 \text{ and } \text{B} = 1.

Dimensional formulas for the given physical quantities:
- (A) Mass density = \text{M}/\text{V} = [\text{M}\text{L}^{-3}\text{T}^0] \rightarrow \text{(IV)}
- (B) Impulse = \text{F}\cdot\Delta t = [\text{M}\text{L}\text{T}^{-2}][\text{T}] = [\text{M}\text{L}\text{T}^{-1}] \rightarrow \text{(II)}
- (C) Power = \text{Work}/\text{Time} = [\text{M}\text{L}^2\text{T}^{-3}] \rightarrow \text{(I)}
- (D) Moment of inertia = \text{M}r^2 = [\text{M}\text{L}^2\text{T}^0] \rightarrow \text{(III)}

Matching: (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III).

From the wave equation y = \sin(kx + \omega t):
k = 20\pi\text{ m}^{-1} \implies \lambda = \frac{2\pi}{k} = \frac{2\pi}{20\pi} = 0.1\text{ m} = 10\text{ cm}

The particle velocity is:
v_p = \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} = 10\pi \cos(20\pi x + 10\pi t)

Oscillating speed is |v_p| = 10\pi |\cos(20\pi x + 10\pi t)|. The minimum separation between two points having the same oscillating speed at any given instant is half the wavelength:
\text{Minimum distance} = \frac{\lambda}{2} = \frac{10\text{ cm}}{2} = 5.0\text{ cm}

Matching thermodynamic processes with their defining properties:
- (A) Isothermal: \Delta\text{T} = 0 \implies \Delta\text{U} = 0 \rightarrow \text{(IV)}
- (B) Adiabatic: No heat exchange, \Delta\text{Q} = 0 \rightarrow \text{(II)}
- (C) Isobaric: Pressure is constant, temperature changes, so \Delta\text{U} \neq 0 \rightarrow \text{(III)}
- (D) Isochoric: Volume is constant (\Delta\text{V} = 0), so \Delta\text{W} = \text{P}\Delta\text{V} = 0 \rightarrow \text{(I)}

Matching: (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I).

Initial horizontal velocity:
u_x = 360 \times \frac{5}{18} = 100\text{ m/s}

Horizontal displacement in t = 20 s:
x = u_x \cdot t = 100 \times 20 = 2000\text{ m} = 2\text{ km}

Vertical altitude:
y = 2\text{ km} = 2000\text{ m}

Total displacement from the release point:
\text{D} = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = \sqrt{2^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2}\text{ km}

Thermal resistance of a rod is given by \text{R}_{\text{th}} = \frac{\text{L}}{\text{KA}} = \frac{\text{L}}{\text{K}\pi r^2}.

The ratio of thermal resistances:
\frac{\text{R}_{A}}{\text{R}_{B}} = (\frac{\text{L}_{A}}{\text{L}_{B}}) \times (\frac{\text{K}_{B}}{\text{K}_{A}}) \times (\frac{r_{B}}{r_{A}})^2 = (\frac{1}{2}) \times (2) \times (\frac{1}{2})^2 = \frac{1}{4}

At steady state, rate of heat flow through rod A equals that through rod B:
\frac{400 - \text{T}}{\text{R}_{A}} = \frac{\text{T} - 200}{\text{R}_{B}}

\frac{400 - \text{T}}{\text{T} - 200} = \frac{\text{R}_{A}}{\text{R}_{B}} = \frac{1}{4}

4(400 - \text{T}) = \text{T} - 200 \implies 1600 - 4\text{T} = \text{T} - 200 \implies 5\text{T} = 1800 \implies \text{T} = 360\text{ K}

Moment of inertia of complete disc of mass \text{M} and radius \text{R} about axis AB through O perpendicular to plane:
\text{I}_1 = \frac{\text{MR}^2}{2}

Mass of the removed circular disc of radius r = \text{R}/3:
m = \text{M} \times \frac{\pi(\text{R}/3)^2}{\pi\text{R}^2} = \frac{\text{M}}{9}

Distance between centre of removed disc \text{O}' and axis through \text{O} is d = \text{R} - \text{R}/3 = \frac{2\text{R}}{3}.

Moment of inertia of removed disc about axis through \text{O} (using parallel axis theorem):
\text{I}_2 = \frac{m r^2}{2} + m d^2 = \frac{(\text{M}/9)(\text{R}/3)^2}{2} + (\frac{\text{M}}{9})(\frac{2\text{R}}{3})^2 = \frac{\text{MR}^2}{162} + \frac{4\text{MR}^2}{81} = \frac{\text{MR}^2 + 8\text{MR}^2}{162} = \frac{9\text{MR}^2}{162} = \frac{\text{MR}^2}{18}

Moment of inertia of remaining portion:
\text{I} = \text{I}_1 - \text{I}_2 = \frac{\text{MR}^2}{2} - \frac{\text{MR}^2}{18} = \frac{8\text{MR}^2}{18} = \frac{4\text{MR}^2}{9}

Comparing with \frac{4}{x}\text{MR}^2 \implies x = 9.

Across a period from left to right, effective nuclear charge (\text{Z}_{\text{eff}}) increases and atomic radius decreases. Therefore, in the 3rd period, atomic radius of magnesium (Group 2) is larger than that of aluminium (Group 13):
r_{Mg} > r_{Al}

Hence, the trend r_{Mg} < r_{Al} is incorrect.

Primary valency = Oxidation State of central metal.
Secondary valency = Coordination Number of central metal.

- (A) [\text{Co}(\text{en})_2\text{Cl}_2]\text{Cl}: Oxidation state = +3, CN = 2(2) + 2 = 6 \rightarrow (3, 6) \rightarrow \text{(I)}
- (B) [\text{Pt}(\text{NH}_3)_2\text{Cl}(\text{NO}_2)]: Oxidation state = +2, CN = 2 + 1 + 1 = 4 \rightarrow (2, 4) \rightarrow \text{(IV)}
- (C) \text{Hg}[\text{Co}(\text{SCN})_4] (assuming \text{Hg}^{1+} or \text{Co}^{3+} in this problem): \text{Co} is +3 or +2, CN = 4 \rightarrow (3, 4) \rightarrow \text{(II)}
- (D) [\text{Mg}(\text{EDTA})]^{2-}: Oxidation state = +2, EDTA is hexadentate (CN = 6) \rightarrow (2, 6) \rightarrow \text{(III)}

Matching: (A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III).

Properties of the binary solutions:
- (A) Chloroform and acetone form intermolecular hydrogen bonds, showing negative deviation from Raoult's law \implies forms Maximum boiling azeotrope \rightarrow \text{(III)}
- (B) Ethanol and water show positive deviation from Raoult's law \implies forms Minimum boiling azeotrope \rightarrow \text{(I)}
- (C) Benzene and toluene form an ideal solution \implies \Delta\text{V}_{\text{mix}} = 0 \rightarrow \text{(IV)}
- (D) Acetic acid in benzene undergoes intermolecular hydrogen bonding to form cyclic dimers \implies Dimerizes \rightarrow \text{(II)}

Matching: (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II).

questions.text:
Given below are two statements :

1 M aqueous solution of each of \text{Cu}(\text{NO}_3)_2, \text{AgNO}_3, \text{Hg}_2(\text{NO}_3)_2; \text{Mg}(\text{NO}_3)_2 are electrolysed using inert electrodes,

Given : \text{E}^\theta_{\text{Ag}^+/\text{Ag}} = 0.80\text{ V}, \text{E}^\theta_{\text{Hg}_2^{2+}/\text{Hg}} = 0.79\text{ V}, \text{E}^\theta_{\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}} = 0.24\text{ V} \text{ and } \text{E}^\theta_{\text{Mg}^{2+}/\text{Mg}} = -2.37\text{ V}

Statement (I) : With increasing voltage, the sequence of deposition of metals on the cathode will be \text{Ag}, \text{Hg} and \text{Cu}

Statement (II) : Magnesium will not be deposited at cathode instead oxygen gas will be evolved at the cathode.

In the light of the above statement, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :

questions.solution:
Pressure of dry nitrogen gas:
\text{P}_{\text{N}_2} = \text{P}_{\text{total}} - \text{Aqueous tension} = 715 - 15 = 700\text{ mm Hg} = \frac{700}{760}\text{ atm}

Moles of \text{N}_2 gas:
n_{\text{N}_2} = \frac{\text{PV}}{\text{RT}} = \frac{(\frac{700}{760}) \times (0.050\text{ L})}{0.0821 \times 300} = \frac{35}{760 \times 24.63} \approx 1.869 \times 10^{-3}\text{ mol}

Mass of nitrogen:
m_{N} = 2 \times n_{\text{N}_2} \times 14 = 28 \times (1.869 \times 10^{-3}) \approx 0.05234\text{ g} = 52.34\text{ mg}

Percentage of nitrogen in the organic compound (292 mg):
\% \text{N} = \frac{52.34\text{ mg}}{292\text{ mg}} \times 100\% \approx 17.92\% \approx 18\%

Molar mass of butane (\text{C}_4\text{H}_{10}) = 4(12) + 10(1) = 58\text{ g/mol}.
Molar mass of \text{O}_2 = 32\text{ g/mol}.

Moles of reactants:
n_{\text{butane}} = \frac{174.0 \times 10^3\text{ g}}{58\text{ g/mol}} = 3.0 \times 10^3\text{ mol}

n_{\text{O}_2} = \frac{320.0 \times 10^3\text{ g}}{32\text{ g/mol}} = 10.0 \times 10^3\text{ mol}

Stoichiometry check for limiting reagent:
- Required \text{O}_2 for 3000 mol butane = 3000 \times 6.5 = 19500\text{ mol}
- Available \text{O}_2 = 10000\text{ mol}
Therefore, \text{O}_2 is the limiting reagent.

Moles of \text{H}_2\text{O} produced:
n_{\text{H}_2\text{O}} = \frac{5}{13/2} \times n_{\text{O}_2} = \frac{10}{13} \times (10 \times 10^3) = \frac{10^5}{13} \approx 7692.3\text{ mol}

Mass of water produced:
m_{\text{H}_2\text{O}} = \frac{10^5}{13} \times 18\text{ g} = \frac{1.8 \times 10^6}{13}\text{ g} \approx 138461.5\text{ g} = 138.46\text{ kg}

Since density of water is 1 g/mL (1 kg/L), volume of water formed is 138.46 L \approx 138 L.

Step-by-step reaction mechanism:
1. \text{Ph}-\text{CO}-\text{CH}_3 + \text{PCl}_5 \xrightarrow{\Delta} \text{Ph}-\text{CCl}_2-\text{CH}_3\text{ (Compound A)}
2. \text{Ph}-\text{CCl}_2-\text{CH}_3 + 3\text{ eq. NaNH}_2\text{ in liquid }\text{NH}_3 \rightarrow \text{Ph}-\text{C}\equiv\text{C}^-\text{Na}^+\text{ (Compound B)}
3. Acidification: \text{Ph}-\text{C}\equiv\text{C}^-\text{Na}^+ + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Ph}-\text{C}\equiv\text{CH}\text{ (Phenylacetylene, Compound C)}
4. Hydroboration-oxidation of terminal alkyne:
\text{Ph}-\text{C}\equiv\text{CH} \xrightarrow{1.\ \text{B}_2\text{H}_6, 2.\ \text{H}_2\text{O}_2, \text{OH}^-} \text{Ph}-\text{CH}=\text{CH-OH} \xrightarrow{\text{tautomerism}} \text{Ph}-\text{CH}_2-\text{CHO}\text{ (Phenylacetaldehyde, Compound D)}

In product D (\text{Ph}-\text{CH}_2-\text{CHO}):
- 6 \text{sp}^2 carbons in the benzene ring (\text{Ph})
- 1 \text{sp}^3 carbon in -\text{CH}_2-
- 1 \text{sp}^2 carbon in the carbonyl group (-\text{CHO})

Total number of \text{sp}^2 hybridised carbon atoms in product D = 6 + 1 = 7.

Applying componendo and dividendo:
\frac{2|\vec{a}+\vec{b}|}{2|\vec{a}-\vec{b}|} = \frac{(\sqrt{2}+1) + 1}{(\sqrt{2}+1) - 1} = \frac{\sqrt{2}+2}{\sqrt{2}} = 1 + \sqrt{2}

Squaring both sides:
\frac{|\vec{a}+\vec{b}|^2}{|\vec{a}-\vec{b}|^2} = (1 + \sqrt{2})^2 = 3 + 2\sqrt{2}

Let |\vec{a}| = |\vec{b}| = k. Then:
|\vec{a}+\vec{b}|^2 = 2k^2 + 2\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}

|\vec{a}-\vec{b}|^2 = 2k^2 - 2\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}

\frac{2k^2 + 2\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}}{2k^2 - 2\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}} = 3 + 2\sqrt{2}

Applying componendo and dividendo again:
\frac{4k^2}{4\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}} = \frac{(3+2\sqrt{2}) + 1}{(3+2\sqrt{2}) - 1} = \frac{4+2\sqrt{2}}{2+2\sqrt{2}} = \frac{2+\sqrt{2}}{1+\sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{2}

\implies \frac{\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}}{k^2} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}

Now, calculate the required ratio:
\frac{|\vec{a}+\vec{b}|^2}{|\vec{a}|^2} = \frac{2k^2 + 2\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}}{k^2} = 2 + 2(\frac{\vec{a}\cdot\vec{b}}{k^2}) = 2 + 2(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}) = 2 + \sqrt{2}

Find the points of intersection:
1. Line x + 7 and line 11 - 3x intersect at x + 7 = 11 - 3x \implies 4x = 4 \implies x = 1 (y = 8).
2. Parabola y = 1 + x^2 and line y = x + 7 intersect at 1 + x^2 = x + 7 \implies x^2 - x - 6 = 0 \implies (x - 3)(x + 2) = 0 \implies x = -2 (for x \le 1).
3. Parabola y = 1 + x^2 and line y = 11 - 3x intersect at 1 + x^2 = 11 - 3x \implies x^2 + 3x - 10 = 0 \implies (x + 5)(x - 2) = 0 \implies x = 2 (for x \ge 1).

The area A is given by:
\text{A} = \int_{-2}^1 ((x + 7) - (1 + x^2))\ dx + \int_1^2 ((11 - 3x) - (1 + x^2))\ dx

\text{A}_1 = \int_{-2}^1 (6 + x - x^2)\ dx = [6x + \frac{x^2}{2} - \frac{x^3}{3}]_{-2}^1 = (6 + \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{3}) - (-12 + 2 + \frac{8}{3}) = \frac{37}{6} - (-\frac{22}{3}) = \frac{37 + 44}{6} = \frac{81}{6} = \frac{27}{2}

\text{A}_2 = \int_1^2 (10 - 3x - x^2)\ dx = [10x - \frac{3x^2}{2} - \frac{x^3}{3}]_1^2 = (20 - 6 - \frac{8}{3}) - (10 - \frac{3}{2} - \frac{1}{3}) = \frac{34}{3} - \frac{49}{6} = \frac{19}{6}

\text{A} = \frac{81}{6} + \frac{19}{6} = \frac{100}{6} = \frac{50}{3}

\implies 3\text{A} = 50

For continuity at x = 0, f(0) = \lim_{x \to 0} f(x).

Rewrite the fraction:
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{(\tan(\tan x) - \tan x) + (\tan x - \sin x) + (\sin x - \sin(\sin x))}{\tan x - \sin x}

Recall the standard limits as u \to 0:
\tan u - u \approx \frac{u^3}{3}

u - \sin u \approx \frac{u^3}{6}

\tan x - \sin x \approx \frac{x^3}{2}

Thus:
- \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\tan(\tan x) - \tan x}{\tan x - \sin x} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\frac{\tan^3 x}{3}}{\frac{x^3}{2}} = \frac{1/3}{1/2} = \frac{2}{3}
- \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\tan x - \sin x}{\tan x - \sin x} = 1
- \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sin x - \sin(\sin x)}{\tan x - \sin x} = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\frac{\sin^3 x}{6}}{\frac{x^3}{2}} = \frac{1/6}{1/2} = \frac{1}{3}

Summing the three parts:
f(0) = \frac{2}{3} + 1 + \frac{1}{3} = 2

Factor out x^{-23} from the 23rd root:
\sqrt[23]{3x^{-24} + x^{-26}} = \sqrt[23]{x^{-23}(3x^{-1} + x^{-3})} = x^{-1} (\frac{3}{x} + \frac{1}{x^3})^{1/23}

The integral becomes:
\text{I} = \int (\frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{x^3}) \frac{1}{x} (\frac{3}{x} + \frac{1}{x^3})^{1/23} dx = \int (\frac{1}{x^2} + \frac{1}{x^4}) (\frac{3}{x} + \frac{1}{x^3})^{1/23} dx

Let t = \frac{3}{x} + \frac{1}{x^3} = 3x^{-1} + x^{-3}.
dt = (-\frac{3}{x^2} - \frac{3}{x^4}) dx = -3(\frac{1}{x^2} + \frac{1}{x^4}) dx \implies (\frac{1}{x^2} + \frac{1}{x^4}) dx = -\frac{dt}{3}

\text{I} = -\frac{1}{3} \int t^{1/23} dt = -\frac{1}{3} \frac{t^{\frac{24}{23}}}{\frac{24}{23}} + \text{C} = -\frac{23}{3(24)} t^{24/23} + \text{C} = -\frac{23}{3(23 + 1)} (3x^{-1} + x^{-3})^{\frac{23+1}{23}} + \text{C}

Comparing with -\frac{\alpha}{3(\alpha + 1)}(3x^\beta + x^\gamma)^{\frac{\alpha+1}{\alpha}} + \text{C}:
\alpha = 23, \beta = -1, \gamma = -3

\alpha + \beta + \gamma = 23 + (-1) + (-3) = 19

1. For a hyperbola with focus (-ae, 0) = (-5, 0) and directrix x = -a/e = -9/5:
ae = 5 \text{ and } \frac{a}{e} = \frac{9}{5}

Multiplying: a^2 = 5 \times \frac{9}{5} = 9 \implies a = 3

Dividing: e^2 = \frac{5}{9/5} = \frac{25}{9} \implies e = \frac{5}{3}

b^2 = a^2(e^2 - 1) = 9(\frac{25}{9} - 1) = 16 \implies b = 4

Equation of the hyperbola: \frac{x^2}{9} - \frac{y^2}{16} = 1.

2. The point (\alpha, 2\sqrt{5}) lies on the hyperbola:
\frac{\alpha^2}{9} - \frac{(2\sqrt{5})^2}{16} = 1 \implies \frac{\alpha^2}{9} - \frac{20}{16} = 1 \implies \frac{\alpha^2}{9} - \frac{5}{4} = 1 \implies \frac{\alpha^2}{9} = \frac{9}{4} \implies \alpha^2 = \frac{81}{4}

3. Product of focal distances of point P(\alpha, y):
p = |e\alpha - a||e\alpha + a| = |e^2 \alpha^2 - a^2| = |\frac{25}{9} \times \frac{81}{4} - 9| = |\frac{225}{4} - 9| = \frac{225 - 36}{4} = \frac{189}{4}

Therefore, 4p = 189.

Consider the binomial expansion:
(1 - x)^{20} = {}^{20}\text{C}_0 - {}^{20}\text{C}_1 x + {}^{20}\text{C}_2 x^2 - {}^{20}\text{C}_3 x^3 + {}^{20}\text{C}_4 x^4 - {}^{20}\text{C}_5 x^5 + \dots + {}^{20}\text{C}_{20} x^{20}

Divide both sides by x^2:
\frac{(1 - x)^{20}}{x^2} = {}^{20}\text{C}_0 x^{-2} - {}^{20}\text{C}_1 x^{-1} + {}^{20}\text{C}_2 - {}^{20}\text{C}_3 x + {}^{20}\text{C}_4 x^2 - {}^{20}\text{C}_5 x^3 + {}^{20}\text{C}_6 x^4 - \dots + {}^{20}\text{C}_{20} x^{18}

Differentiating twice with respect to x:
First derivative:
\frac{d}{dx}[\frac{(1 - x)^{20}}{x^2}] = -2{}^{20}\text{C}_0 x^{-3} + {}^{20}\text{C}_1 x^{-2} - {}^{20}\text{C}_3 + 2{}^{20}\text{C}_4 x - 3{}^{20}\text{C}_5 x^2 + 4{}^{20}\text{C}_6 x^3 - \dots

Second derivative:
\frac{d^2}{dx^2}[\frac{(1 - x)^{20}}{x^2}] = 6{}^{20}\text{C}_0 x^{-4} - 2{}^{20}\text{C}_1 x^{-3} + 2 \times 1 \times {}^{20}\text{C}_4 - 3 \times 2 \times {}^{20}\text{C}_5 x + 4 \times 3 \times {}^{20}\text{C}_6 x^2 - \dots + 18 \times 17 \times {}^{20}\text{C}_{20} x^{16}

At x = 1, since the term (1 - x)^{20} has factor (1 - x)^{18} after differentiating twice by product rule, the LHS evaluates to 0:
0 = 6{}^{20}\text{C}_0(1) - 2{}^{20}\text{C}_1(1) + \text{S}

0 = 6(1) - 2(20) + \text{S} = 6 - 40 + \text{S} = -34 + \text{S}

\implies \text{S} = 34
